@@s039 Points that the official documentation does not yet clarify.
@@s040 How to read the atlas
@@s041 The explanations are <b>sheets</b> written one step at a time (Forward, or the arrows <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> after clicking on the sheet). The colours always have the same role:
@@s042 The typical tool of this atlas is the <b>interleaving builder</b>: you are the scheduler and decide which thread goes ahead. Try it: two threads do <code>counter++</code>, written as two steps. Can you get <code>counter = 1</code>?
@@s043 Two threads, one counter
@@s044 Map of the topics
@@s045 For the exam
@@s046 What you must be able to do at the end
@@s047 From the official description, in simple words:
@@s048 <b>Analyse</b> the correctness of concurrent Java software and connect it to the Java Memory Model.
@@s049 <b>Analyse</b> the performance of concurrent software.
@@s050 <b>Use</b> threads, locks, <code>final</code> and <code>volatile</code> fields and concurrent collections to build correct and fast software.
@@s051 <b>Use</b> tools to test and analyse concurrency problems.
@@s052 <b>Compare</b> shared memory and message passing.
@@s053 <b>Apply</b> the actor model in Erlang.
@@s054 <b>Describe</b> the differences between multicore CPUs and GPUs and the consequences for performance.
@@s055 Estimated time: 30% assignments, 30% preparation, 16% exam, 12% lectures, 12% exercises. Generative AI: allowed during the course as a study tool, forbidden at the exam.
